\DOC simp_ss

\TYPE {simp_ss : unit -> simpset}

\SYNOPSIS
The simp set as a HOL Light simpset.

\DESCRIBE
A call {simp_ss ()} returns the declared rules as a {simpset}: HOL
Light's {basic_ss} extended with everything that {add_simp} has been
given. It is what {SIMPS_CONV} runs, and it is there for a proof that
wants one of the other simplification entry points -- {ONCE_SIMPLIFY_CONV},
a different prover for the conditions, or a simpset of its own built on
top of it.

The simpset is extended as rules are declared, not built on demand, so
asking for it costs nothing.

\FAILURE
Never fails.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # SIMPLIFY_CONV (simp_ss ()) [ADD_CLAUSES] `count (Call Nil)`;;
  val it : thm = |- count (Call Nil) = 0
}

\SEEALSO
add_simp, basic_ss, SIMPLIFY_CONV, simps, SIMPS_CONV, ss_of_thms.

\ENDDOC
